{
  "dom_id": "step-q109800",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,…$\n\nII. $-5,\\,10,\\,-20,\\,…$\n\nIII. $1,\\,-4,\\,-16,\\,…$",
  "worked_solution": "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{10}{4}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{(-5)}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-20)}{10}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{(-4)}{1}=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-16)}{(-4)}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequence II could be a geometric sequence.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
